    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 9 Atoms and Formulae|Atomic mass and molecular mass",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Molecular mass is the sum of the relative atomic masses of all atoms in a molecule.\n\nMass number is the total number of protons and neutrons in one atom's nucleus.",
        "The molecular formula tells how many of each atom to include. Relative molecular mass is dimensionless; molecular mass is often expressed in unified atomic mass units.\n\nIt is a whole number written A. Electrons contribute so little mass that they are not counted in the mass number.",
        "H2O has molecular mass 2(1.008) + 16.00 = 18.016 u.\n\nExample: sodium-23 has 11 protons and 12 neutrons, so A = 11 + 12 = 23.",
        "molecular mass = sum of atomic masses in the molecular formula\n\nA = Z + N",
        "Remember to multiply each atomic mass by its subscript.\n\nMass number for one isotope is not the same as the decimal relative atomic mass on a periodic table.",
        "Molecular mass is used to compare molecules and convert between mass and amount of substance.\n\nMass number helps identify isotopes and calculate neutron numbers.",
        listOf("H2O has molecular mass 2(1.008) + 16.00 = 18.016 u.","Example: sodium-23 has 11 protons and 12 neutrons, so A = 11 + 12 = 23.","molecular mass = sum of atomic masses in the molecular formula\n\nA = Z + N","The molecular formula tells how many of each atom to include.","Relative molecular mass is dimensionless","molecular mass is often expressed in unified atomic mass units."),
        listOf("Atomic mass","Molecular formula","Molar mass","Protons and neutrons","Isotopes","Nuclear notation","Molecular mass","Mass number")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 9 Atoms and Formulae|Valency and writing formulae",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Valency is an atom's combining capacity, often related to electrons lost, gained or shared.\n\nA chemical formula uses element symbols and subscripts to show a substance's composition.",
        "For simple main-group compounds, valency helps predict how many bonds an atom forms or the charge of its common ion.\n\nSubscripts tell the relative number of atoms or ions; coefficients count whole particles and must not be confused with subscripts.",
        "magnesium has valency 2 and chlorine has valency 1, giving MgCl2 so the combining capacities balance.\n\nExample: Al3+ and O2- combine in the smallest neutral ratio to give Al2O3.",
        "total positive combining capacity = total negative combining capacity\n\nsum of ionic charges in a neutral formula = 0",
        "Valency is not always identical to oxidation state, especially for elements with variable behavior.\n\nDo not change subscripts to balance a reaction; that would change the substances.",
        "Valency helps learners write formulas and understand common bonding patterns.\n\nFormulas provide a compact language for labels, equations, recipes and safety information.",
        listOf("magnesium has valency 2 and chlorine has valency 1, giving MgCl2 so the combining capacities balance.","Example: Al3+ and O2- combine in the smallest neutral ratio to give Al2O3.","total positive combining capacity = total negative combining capacity\n\nsum of ionic charges in a neutral formula = 0","For simple main-group compounds, valency helps predict how many bonds an atom forms or the charge of its common ion.","Subscripts tell the relative number of atoms or ions","coefficients count whole particles and must not be confused with subscripts."),
        listOf("Valence electrons","Ion charge","Chemical formulas","Element symbols","Valency","Formula units","Chemical formula")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 9 Atoms and Formulae|Mole concept introduction",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "The mole is the SI unit for amount of substance.\n\nAvogadro's number is the number of specified particles in one mole: exactly 6.02214076 × 10²³ mol⁻¹.",
        "One mole contains Avogadro's number of specified entities. Molar mass converts between amount in moles and measured mass.\n\nIt links the microscopic count of atoms, molecules or ions to laboratory-scale amounts.",
        "18.0 g of water divided by its molar mass, 18.0 g/mol, equals 1.00 mol H2O.\n\nExample: 0.50 mol of oxygen molecules contains 0.50 × 6.022 × 10²³ = 3.011 × 10²³ O₂ molecules.",
        "moles = mass / molar mass\n\nnumber of particles = moles × Avogadro constant",
        "Do not use grams directly as equation coefficients; balanced equations compare amounts in moles.\n\nAlways state the entity being counted; one mole of O atoms differs from one mole of O2 molecules.",
        "Moles allow chemists to scale reactions, formulations and material compositions.\n\nThe constant connects measured masses with particle counts in reactions and materials.",
        listOf("18.0 g of water divided by its molar mass, 18.0 g/mol, equals 1.00 mol H2O.","Example: 0.50 mol of oxygen molecules contains 0.50 × 6.022 × 10²³ = 3.011 × 10²³ O₂ molecules.","moles = mass / molar mass\n\nnumber of particles = moles × Avogadro constant","One mole contains Avogadro's number of specified entities.","Molar mass converts between amount in moles and measured mass.","It links the microscopic count of atoms, molecules or ions to laboratory-scale amounts."),
        listOf("Avogadro number","Molar mass","Stoichiometry","Moles","Particles")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 9 Atoms and Formulae|Formation of ionic and covalent compounds",
        "existing authored record",
        "Ionic compounds form through electron transfer and electrostatic attraction, while covalent compounds form through sharing electron pairs.",
        "Metals commonly lose valence electrons to form cations and non-metals gain them to form anions. Between non-metals, shared pairs allow atoms to approach stable outer-shell configurations. Charge balance determines ionic formulae; shared-pair count determines covalent structure.",
        "Magnesium loses two electrons and two chlorine atoms each gain one, forming Mg2+ and 2Cl-, hence MgCl2. Two hydrogen atoms share one pair to form H2.",
        "ionic: Na -> Na+ + e- and Cl + e- -> Cl-; covalent: H:H",
        "Do not describe an ionic compound as a molecule made of one ion pair; an ionic solid contains a repeating lattice of oppositely charged ions.",
        "Bond type helps predict structure, conductivity, melting point, solubility and material use.",
        listOf("Magnesium loses two electrons and two chlorine atoms each gain one, forming Mg2+ and 2Cl-, hence MgCl2.","Two hydrogen atoms share one pair to form H2.","ionic: Na -> Na+ + e- and Cl + e- -> Cl-; covalent: H:H","Metals commonly lose valence electrons to form cations and non-metals gain them to form anions.","Between non-metals, shared pairs allow atoms to approach stable outer-shell configurations.","Charge balance determines ionic formulae"),
        listOf("Valency","Electron transfer","Electron sharing")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 9 Atoms and Formulae|Properties of ionic versus covalent compounds",
        "existing authored record",
        "Ionic and covalent compounds show different bulk properties because their particles and bonding structures differ.",
        "Ionic solids usually have high melting points, are brittle and conduct only when molten or dissolved because ions must move. Simple covalent substances often have lower melting/boiling points and usually do not conduct because they lack mobile charged particles. Giant covalent structures such as diamond are important exceptions.",
        "Solid NaCl does not light a conductivity tester, but molten or aqueous NaCl can. Sugar solution generally does not conduct strongly because sugar remains as neutral molecules.",
        "mobile ions -> electrical conduction; fixed lattice ions -> no solid-state conduction",
        "Do not claim every covalent substance has a low melting point or is insoluble in water; structure, polarity and intermolecular forces create exceptions.",
        "Comparing compound properties guides material selection, electrolyte use and safe interpretation of laboratory tests.",
        listOf("Solid NaCl does not light a conductivity tester, but molten or aqueous NaCl can.","Sugar solution generally does not conduct strongly because sugar remains as neutral molecules.","mobile ions -> electrical conduction; fixed lattice ions -> no solid-state conduction","Ionic solids usually have high melting points, are brittle and conduct only when molten or dissolved because ions must move.","Simple covalent substances often have lower melting/boiling points and usually do not conduct because they lack mobile charged particles.","Giant covalent structures such as diamond are important exceptions."),
        listOf("Particle mobility","Melting point","Electrical conductivity")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 9 Atoms and Formulae|Electron-dot structures",
        "existing authored record",
        "Electron-dot structures show valence electrons and the pairs transferred or shared during compound formation.",
        "Write the correct number of valence dots around each element symbol. For covalent molecules, place shared pairs between bonded atoms and lone pairs on the appropriate atoms. For ionic compounds, show bracketed ions with charges after electron transfer.",
        "In H2O, oxygen shares one pair with each hydrogen and retains two lone pairs. For NaCl, show [Na]+ and [Cl]- with chlorine's complete octet.",
        "one shared pair = one covalent bond; bracket plus charge = ion",
        "Do not draw core electrons or omit ionic charges; electron-dot structures focus on valence electrons and must conserve the total electron count.",
        "Dot structures provide a bridge from valency and formulas to bonding, shape and chemical reactivity.",
        listOf("In H2O, oxygen shares one pair with each hydrogen and retains two lone pairs.","For NaCl, show [Na]+ and [Cl]- with chlorine's complete octet.","one shared pair = one covalent bond; bracket plus charge = ion","Write the correct number of valence dots around each element symbol.","For covalent molecules, place shared pairs between bonded atoms and lone pairs on the appropriate atoms.","For ionic compounds, show bracketed ions with charges after electron transfer."),
        listOf("Valence electrons","Lewis symbols","Octet rule")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 10 Chemical Substances and Carbon|Chemical equations and conservation",
        "new case",
        "A balanced chemical equation expresses atom conservation using coefficients rather than altered formulas.",
        "Subscripts define chemical identity; coefficients change the number of formula units or molecules. State symbols add physical information without changing atom counts.",
        "Balancing hydrogen combustion gives 2H2 + O2 -> 2H2O. Each side has four hydrogen atoms and two oxygen atoms; changing water to H2O2 would describe a different product rather than balance the same reaction.",
        "balance coefficients while preserving chemical formulas",
