    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 10 Chemical Substances and Carbon|Important salts in daily life",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Baking soda is sodium hydrogen carbonate, NaHCO3, prepared in the Solvay process and decomposed by heat to release carbon dioxide.\n\nWashing soda is crystalline sodium carbonate decahydrate, Na2CO3·10H2O, obtained by crystallising sodium carbonate with water.",
        "Concentrated brine is saturated with ammonia and carbon dioxide so sparingly soluble NaHCO3 precipitates. Gentle heating releases CO2, which expands dough; the mild base also neutralises acids.\n\nHeating sodium hydrogen carbonate forms sodium carbonate, which crystallises from water as the decahydrate. Carbonate ions precipitate calcium and magnesium ions, allowing washing soda to soften hard water.",
        "In baking powder, NaHCO3 reacts with an edible acid to release CO2. Tartaric acid helps prevent the bitter taste that excess sodium carbonate could cause.\n\nExample: In hard water, Ca2+ + CO3^2- -> CaCO3(s), removing calcium ions that otherwise interfere with soap.",
        "NaCl + H2O + CO2 + NH3 -> NH4Cl + NaHCO3; 2NaHCO3 --heat--> Na2CO3 + CO2 + H2O\n\n2NaHCO3 --heat--> Na2CO3 + CO2 + H2O; Na2CO3 + 10H2O -> Na2CO3·10H2O",
        "Do not identify baking soda as sodium carbonate; it is sodium hydrogen carbonate and has different formula and behaviour.\n\nDo not confuse anhydrous sodium carbonate with washing-soda crystals; the named school compound contains ten waters of crystallisation.",
        "Baking soda is used in baking powder, antacids and certain fire extinguishers.\n\nWashing soda is used for cleaning, water softening and manufacture of glass, soap and paper.",
        listOf("In baking powder, NaHCO3 reacts with an edible acid to release CO2.","Tartaric acid helps prevent the bitter taste that excess sodium carbonate could cause.","Example: In hard water, Ca2+ + CO3^2- -> CaCO3(s), removing calcium ions that otherwise interfere with soap.","NaCl + H2O + CO2 + NH3 -> NH4Cl + NaHCO3; 2NaHCO3 --heat--> Na2CO3 + CO2 + H2O\n\n2NaHCO3 --heat--> Na2CO3 + CO2 + H2O; Na2CO3 + 10H2O -> Na2CO3·10H2O","Concentrated brine is saturated with ammonia and carbon dioxide so sparingly soluble NaHCO3 precipitates.","Gentle heating releases CO2, which expands dough"),
        listOf("Solvay process","Thermal decomposition","Carbon dioxide","Water of crystallisation","Hard water","Sodium carbonate","Preparation equations and uses of baking soda","Preparation equations and uses of washing soda")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 10 Chemical Substances and Carbon|Metals, metallurgy and corrosion",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "A metal's position in the reactivity series determines whether it is extracted by electrolysis, chemical reduction or heating of its ore.\n\nCorrosion is unwanted electrochemical oxidation of a material by its environment.",
        "Highly reactive K-Ca-Na-Mg-Al metals require electrolysis of molten compounds. Middle metals such as Zn, Fe, Pb and Cu are commonly obtained after converting ores to oxides and reducing with carbon/CO or aluminium. Low-reactivity metals may occur native or be recovered by heating.\n\nFor iron rusting, anodic iron dissolution and cathodic oxygen reduction occur at different surface regions; water and ions complete the circuit.",
        "ZnS is roasted: 2ZnS + 3O2 -> 2ZnO + 2SO2; then ZnO + C -> Zn + CO. Aluminium oxide instead requires molten electrolysis.\n\nExample: zinc coating protects steel both as a barrier and, if scratched, by oxidizing sacrificially before iron.",
        "high activity -> electrolysis; middle -> roasting/calcination then reduction; low -> heating or native occurrence\n\nanode: Fe -> Fe2+ + 2e-",
        "Do not propose carbon reduction for sodium or aluminium oxides; these metals are more reactive than carbon.\n\nRusting requires an electrochemical pathway; oxygen alone without suitable moisture is insufficient for ordinary rust formation.",
        "Selecting extraction routes balances chemical feasibility, energy demand, ore composition, pollution control and recycling.\n\nCorrosion science protects bridges, pipelines, vehicles, ships and electronic equipment.",
        listOf("ZnS is roasted: 2ZnS + 3O2 -> 2ZnO + 2SO2","then ZnO + C -> Zn + CO.","Aluminium oxide instead requires molten electrolysis.","Example: zinc coating protects steel both as a barrier and, if scratched, by oxidizing sacrificially before iron.","high activity -> electrolysis; middle -> roasting/calcination then reduction; low -> heating or native occurrence\n\nanode: Fe -> Fe2+ + 2e-","Highly reactive K-Ca-Na-Mg-Al metals require electrolysis of molten compounds."),
        listOf("Reactivity series","Metallurgy","Electrolysis and reduction","Sacrificial protection","Galvanization","Electrochemical cells","Metal extraction by reactivity series","Corrosion")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 10 Chemical Substances and Carbon|Carbon bonding and homologous series",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "A covalent bond forms when atoms share one or more pairs of electrons.\n\nHydrocarbons are classified as saturated, unsaturated, aliphatic, alicyclic or aromatic and named from parent chain, unsaturation and substituents.",
        "Shared electron density attracts both nuclei. Single, double and triple bonds differ in shared pairs, length and strength.\n\nIUPAC naming prioritises the longest chain containing maximum multiple bonds and assigns the lowest valid set of locants. A board-ready explanation should connect the stated relationship with electronic structure, a measurable trend, a balanced transformation or laboratory evidence, and should name important exceptions instead of treating the rule as absolute.",
        "in H2, each hydrogen contributes one electron to a shared pair, allowing both atoms to achieve a stable duet.\n\nExample: CH3CH(CH3)CH2CH3 is 2-methylbutane after selecting the longest chain and lowest locant. Show the governing relationship, apply the stated conditions and finish by interpreting the chemical result.",
        "bond order: single < double < triple\n\nalkane CnH2n+2; alkene CnH2n; alkyne CnH2n-2",
        "Sharing is not always perfectly equal; electronegativity differences can make a covalent bond polar.\n\nDo not answer Hydrocarbon classification and nomenclature from a memorised trend or name alone. Check the species, physical state, units, reaction conditions and known exceptions before drawing the conclusion.",
        "Covalent bonding creates molecules, polymers, biomolecules and network solids.\n\nHydrocarbon classification and nomenclature supports prediction and problem solving in senior-secondary chemistry, laboratory work, materials, environmental decisions and industrial chemical processes.",
        listOf("in H2, each hydrogen contributes one electron to a shared pair, allowing both atoms to achieve a stable duet.","Example: CH3CH(CH3)CH2CH3 is 2-methylbutane after selecting the longest chain and lowest locant.","Show the governing relationship, apply the stated conditions and finish by interpreting the chemical result.","bond order: single < double < triple\n\nalkane CnH2n+2; alkene CnH2n; alkyne CnH2n-2","Shared electron density attracts both nuclei.","Single, double and triple bonds differ in shared pairs, length and strength."),
        listOf("Electronegativity","Lewis structures","Bond polarity","Structure-property relationship","Experimental evidence","Board-style calculation or equation","Covalent bond","Hydrocarbon classification and nomenclature")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 10 Chemical Substances and Carbon|Ethanol, ethanoic acid, soaps and detergents",
        "explicit topic alias",
        "Alcohols are prepared by hydration or reduction and react through the O-H bond and C-O bond in oxidation, dehydration, esterification and substitution.\n\nCarboxylic acids form by oxidation or hydrolysis and react through acidity and nucleophilic acyl substitution to make salts, esters, acyl halides and amides.\n\nSoap ions have a water-attracting ionic head and an oil-attracting hydrocarbon tail, allowing them to surround grease in micelles.",
        "Alkenes hydrate to alcohols; aldehydes and ketones reduce to primary and secondary alcohols. Primary alcohol oxidation gives aldehydes then acids, secondary gives ketones, while tertiary alcohols resist mild oxidation. Concentrated acid causes dehydration, and carboxylic acids give esters reversibly.\n\nOxidation of primary alcohols or aldehydes and hydrolysis of nitriles produce acids. Resonance stabilises carboxylate ions. Acids react with bicarbonate releasing CO2, esterify alcohols reversibly, and decarboxylate as sodium salts with soda lime. Electron-withdrawing substituents increase acidity.\n\nAbove a suitable concentration in water, tails cluster inward around oily dirt while charged heads face outward. Agitation breaks grease into suspended droplets that can be rinsed away.",
        "CH3CH2OH + [O] -> CH3CHO + H2O -> CH3COOH. With ethanoic acid and acid catalyst it forms ethyl ethanoate and water.\n\nExample: CH3COOH + NaHCO3 -> CH3COONa + CO2 + H2O. CH3COOH + C2H5OH <=> CH3COOC2H5 + H2O with H+ and heat.\n\nExample: When oily cloth is washed with soap, micelles encapsulate oil and keep droplets dispersed instead of redepositing them on the fabric.",
        "RCH2OH + [O] -> RCHO -> RCOOH; ROH + R'COOH <=> R'COOR + H2O\n\nRCOOH + ROH <=> RCOOR + H2O; RCOONa + NaOH -> RH + Na2CO3\n\nhydrophobic tails -> grease core; hydrophilic COO- heads -> water",
        "Do not state that all alcohols oxidise to carboxylic acids; the product depends on whether the alcohol is primary, secondary or tertiary.\n\nDo not confuse alcohol acidity with carboxylic-acid acidity or forget that Fischer esterification is an equilibrium requiring acid catalyst.\n\nDo not say soap chemically destroys all grease; its principal cleansing action emulsifies and suspends oily material.",
        "Alcohol reactions support solvents, fuels, antiseptics, fragrances, polymers and pharmaceutical synthesis.\n\nCarboxylic-acid derivatives occur in medicines, polymers, food preservatives, fats, soaps and fragrances.\n\nMicelles enable washing, emulsification and formulation of cleaners and personal-care products.",
        listOf("CH3CH2OH + [O] -> CH3CHO + H2O -> CH3COOH.","With ethanoic acid and acid catalyst it forms ethyl ethanoate and water.","Example: CH3COOH + NaHCO3 -> CH3COONa + CO2 + H2O.","CH3COOH + C2H5OH <=> CH3COOC2H5 + H2O with H+ and heat.","Example: When oily cloth is washed with soap, micelles encapsulate oil and keep droplets dispersed instead of redepositing them on the fabric.","RCH2OH + [O] -> RCHO -> RCOOH; ROH + R'COOH <=> R'COOR + H2O\n\nRCOOH + ROH <=> RCOOR + H2O; RCOONa + NaOH -> RH + Na2CO3\n\nhydrophobic tails -> grease core; hydrophilic COO- heads -> water"),
        listOf("Oxidation","Dehydration","Esterification","Acidity","Decarboxylation","Amphiphiles","Emulsification","Surface chemistry","Preparation and reactions of alcohols","Preparation and reactions of carboxylic acids","Soap micelles and cleansing action")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 10 Chemical Substances and Carbon|Balancing chemical equations",
        "existing authored record",
        "A balanced chemical equation contains equal numbers of every type of atom on the reactant and product sides.",
        "Write correct formulae first, count atoms, adjust whole-number coefficients and reduce them to the smallest ratio. Coefficients multiply complete formula units; subscripts belong to substance identities and must not be altered during balancing.",
        "For Al + O2 -> Al2O3, balance aluminium and oxygen to obtain 4Al + 3O2 -> 2Al2O3, then verify four Al and six O atoms on each side.",
        "unbalanced: Fe + H2O -> Fe3O4 + H2; balanced: 3Fe + 4H2O -> Fe3O4 + 4H2",
        "Never change a subscript such as H2O into H2O2 to balance atoms; that changes water into a different substance.",
        "Balanced equations support quantitative reaction calculations, manufacturing yields and pollution accounting.",
        listOf("For Al + O2 -> Al2O3, balance aluminium and oxygen to obtain 4Al + 3O2 -> 2Al2O3, then verify four Al and six O atoms on each side.","unbalanced: Fe + H2O -> Fe3O4 + H2; balanced: 3Fe + 4H2O -> Fe3O4 + 4H2","Write correct formulae first, count atoms, adjust whole-number coefficients and reduce them to the smallest ratio.","Coefficients multiply complete formula units","subscripts belong to substance identities and must not be altered during balancing.","Never change a subscript such as H2O into H2O2 to balance atoms; that changes water into a different substance."),
        listOf("Conservation of mass","Coefficients","Stoichiometry")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 10 Chemical Substances and Carbon|State symbols and reaction observations",
        "existing authored record",
        "State symbols and observation notes connect a symbolic equation with what is actually seen during a reaction.",
        "Use (s), (l), (g) and (aq) for solid, liquid, gas and aqueous solution. Record evidence such as colour change, gas evolution, precipitate formation, temperature change, light or odour without confusing observation with particle-level inference.",
        "Mixing colourless silver-nitrate and sodium-chloride solutions produces a white precipitate. 'White solid appears' is the observation; 'AgCl formed' is the chemical inference.",
        "CaCO3(s) --heat--> CaO(s) + CO2(g); AgNO3(aq) + NaCl(aq) -> AgCl(s)↓ + NaNO3(aq)",
        "Do not write (aq) for every liquid: aqueous means dissolved in water, while pure liquid water is marked (l).",
        "Clear observation records help identify substances, verify reactions and communicate laboratory results safely.",
        listOf("Mixing colourless silver-nitrate and sodium-chloride solutions produces a white precipitate. 'White solid appears' is the observation","'AgCl formed' is the chemical inference.","CaCO3(s) --heat--> CaO(s) + CO2(g); AgNO3(aq) + NaCl(aq) -> AgCl(s)↓ + NaNO3(aq)","Use (s), (l), (g) and (aq) for solid, liquid, gas and aqueous solution.","Record evidence such as colour change, gas evolution, precipitate formation, temperature change, light or odour without confusing observation with particle-level inference.","Do not write (aq) for every liquid: aqueous means dissolved in water, while pure liquid water is marked (l)."),
        listOf("Physical states","Laboratory evidence","Precipitates and gases")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 10 Chemical Substances and Carbon|Endothermic and exothermic reactions",
        "existing authored record",
        "Exothermic reactions transfer heat to the surroundings, while endothermic reactions absorb heat from the surroundings.",
        "In an exothermic process the surroundings warm and products have lower enthalpy than reactants; in an endothermic process the surroundings cool and products have higher enthalpy. Activation energy is still required to begin either type.",
        "Combustion of methane, CH4 + 2O2 -> CO2 + 2H2O + heat, is exothermic. Thermal decomposition of CaCO3 requires heat and is endothermic.",
        "exothermic: reactants -> products + heat; endothermic: reactants + heat -> products",
        "A reaction that needs ignition is not necessarily endothermic; combustion needs activation energy but releases more energy overall.",
        "Heat classification explains fuels, respiration, cold packs, thermal decomposition and energy-efficient industrial reactions.",
        listOf("Combustion of methane, CH4 + 2O2 -> CO2 + 2H2O + heat, is exothermic.","Thermal decomposition of CaCO3 requires heat and is endothermic.","exothermic: reactants -> products + heat; endothermic: reactants + heat -> products","In an exothermic process the surroundings warm and products have lower enthalpy than reactants","in an endothermic process the surroundings cool and products have higher enthalpy.","Activation energy is still required to begin either type."),
        listOf("Energy profiles","Combustion","Thermal decomposition")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 10 Chemical Substances and Carbon|Precipitation reactions",
        "existing authored record",
        "A precipitation reaction forms an insoluble solid when ions from aqueous solutions combine.",
        "Double-displacement reactions exchange ions, but a reaction is driven only when a precipitate, gas or weakly ionised product forms. The net ionic equation removes spectator ions and shows the species responsible for the visible solid.",
        "Pb(NO3)2(aq) + 2KI(aq) -> PbI2(s)↓ + 2KNO3(aq) gives a yellow lead-iodide precipitate.",
        "Ag+(aq) + Cl-(aq) -> AgCl(s)↓",
        "Do not call every double-displacement mixture a precipitation reaction; first check whether an insoluble product actually forms.",
        "Precipitation is used in ion identification, water treatment, pigment preparation and removal of contaminants.",
        listOf("Pb(NO3)2(aq) + 2KI(aq) -> PbI2(s)↓ + 2KNO3(aq) gives a yellow lead-iodide precipitate.","Ag+(aq) + Cl-(aq) -> AgCl(s)↓","Double-displacement reactions exchange ions, but a reaction is driven only when a precipitate, gas or weakly ionised product forms.","The net ionic equation removes spectator ions and shows the species responsible for the visible solid.","Do not call every double-displacement mixture a precipitation reaction; first check whether an insoluble product actually forms."),
        listOf("Double displacement","Solubility","Net ionic equations")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 10 Chemical Substances and Carbon|Preparation equations and uses of sodium hydroxide",
        "existing authored record",
        "Sodium hydroxide is manufactured by electrolysis of concentrated aqueous sodium chloride in the chlor-alkali process.",
        "At the anode chloride ions form chlorine; at the cathode water forms hydrogen and hydroxide ions. Sodium ions remain with hydroxide in solution. Chlorine and hydrogen must be kept separated because their mixture is hazardous.",
        "Electrolysis produces NaOH solution together with chlorine for disinfectants/PVC and hydrogen for fuels or chemical synthesis.",
        "2NaCl(aq) + 2H2O(l) -> 2NaOH(aq) + Cl2(g) + H2(g)",
        "Do not say sodium metal is deposited from brine; water is reduced preferentially at the cathode under these aqueous conditions.",
        "Sodium hydroxide is used in soaps, paper, detergents, rayon, degreasing and neutralisation, with strict corrosive-chemical controls.",
        listOf("Electrolysis produces NaOH solution together with chlorine for disinfectants/PVC and hydrogen for fuels or chemical synthesis.","2NaCl(aq) + 2H2O(l) -> 2NaOH(aq) + Cl2(g) + H2(g)","At the anode chloride ions form chlorine","at the cathode water forms hydrogen and hydroxide ions.","Sodium ions remain with hydroxide in solution.","Chlorine and hydrogen must be kept separated because their mixture is hazardous."),
        listOf("Chlor-alkali process","Electrolysis","Industrial chemicals")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 10 Chemical Substances and Carbon|Preparation equations and uses of bleaching powder",
        "existing authored record",
        "Bleaching powder is produced by passing chlorine over dry slaked lime and is commonly represented as calcium oxychloride, CaOCl2.",
        "The product releases oxidising chlorine species in moist conditions, accounting for bleaching and disinfection. Dry reactants, controlled chlorine exposure and safe storage are important because acids can liberate toxic chlorine gas.",
        "Chlorine from the chlor-alkali process reacts with slaked lime to make bleaching powder used to disinfect drinking water and bleach cotton or paper pulp.",
        "Ca(OH)2 + Cl2 -> CaOCl2 + H2O",
        "Do not mix bleaching powder with acids or ammonia-containing cleaners; dangerous chlorine or chloramine gases may form.",
        "Bleaching powder supports water sanitation, textile processing and oxidising treatments.",
        listOf("Chlorine from the chlor-alkali process reacts with slaked lime to make bleaching powder used to disinfect drinking water and bleach cotton or paper pulp.","Ca(OH)2 + Cl2 -> CaOCl2 + H2O","The product releases oxidising chlorine species in moist conditions, accounting for bleaching and disinfection.","Dry reactants, controlled chlorine exposure and safe storage are important because acids can liberate toxic chlorine gas.","Do not mix bleaching powder with acids or ammonia-containing cleaners; dangerous chlorine or chloramine gases may form."),
        listOf("Available chlorine","Oxidation","Water treatment")),
    EditorialLessonRecord("Class 9–10|Class 10 Chemical Substances and Carbon|Preparation equations and uses of baking soda",
        "existing authored record",
        "Baking soda is sodium hydrogen carbonate, NaHCO3, prepared in the Solvay process and decomposed by heat to release carbon dioxide.",
        "Concentrated brine is saturated with ammonia and carbon dioxide so sparingly soluble NaHCO3 precipitates. Gentle heating releases CO2, which expands dough; the mild base also neutralises acids.",
        "In baking powder, NaHCO3 reacts with an edible acid to release CO2. Tartaric acid helps prevent the bitter taste that excess sodium carbonate could cause.",
        "NaCl + H2O + CO2 + NH3 -> NH4Cl + NaHCO3; 2NaHCO3 --heat--> Na2CO3 + CO2 + H2O",
        "Do not identify baking soda as sodium carbonate; it is sodium hydrogen carbonate and has different formula and behaviour.",
        "Baking soda is used in baking powder, antacids and certain fire extinguishers.",
        listOf("In baking powder, NaHCO3 reacts with an edible acid to release CO2.","Tartaric acid helps prevent the bitter taste that excess sodium carbonate could cause.","NaCl + H2O + CO2 + NH3 -> NH4Cl + NaHCO3; 2NaHCO3 --heat--> Na2CO3 + CO2 + H2O","Concentrated brine is saturated with ammonia and carbon dioxide so sparingly soluble NaHCO3 precipitates.","Gentle heating releases CO2, which expands dough","the mild base also neutralises acids."),
        listOf("Solvay process","Thermal decomposition","Carbon dioxide")),
